L000373	\textbf{Higher-$k$ cases.} Starting from k=6, the topological phase is no longer given by a finite-group gauge theory. The symmetry permutes two self-bosonic anyons. Except for special values of $k$ (e.g., $k =10, 26, 28$ for $k<30$), the permuted anyons always have non-trivial mutual braiding. In this regard, the symmetry might be considered as EM exchange symmetry in a loose sense. Nevertheless, when k = 10, 26 or 28, there are fusion channels of the two self-bosonic anyons that braid trivially. In such cases, we can only say that the symmetry always permutes two self-bosonic anyons that \textit{can} braid non-trivially, i.e., there always exist fusion channels that the two braid non-trivially.
